cs1,0$ 
s"Bird's Nest"
1,0 $ Fruit body tiny (1.5-3 mm wide), almost spherical at first; white to orange-yellow; opening to form 4-9star-shaped rays and exposing a single, dark spore ball (peridiole) which may be catapulted a distanceof more than 6 m $ Sphaerobolus stellatus
1,2 $ Fruit body larger, typically 6-10 mm wide and high; numerous “eggs”(peridioles) contained in eachcup (“nest”)
2,0 $ Peridioles white; interior of cup smooth $ Crucibulum laeve
2,3 $ Peridioles gray to grayish-brown to blackish
3,0 $ Cup interior vertically lined or grooved; cup exterior shaggy-hairy $ Cyathus striatus
3,4 $ Cup interior smooth; cup exterior finely hairy to shaggy-hairy
4,0 $ Peridioles 2-3.5 mm wide; cup exterior finely hairy, velvety; mouth of cupflaring, margin often wavy $ Cyathus olla
4,0 $ Peridioles 1-2 mm wide; cup exterior shaggy-hairy (wooly) atleast when young; mouth of cup not conspicuously flaring, margin typically circular $ Cyathus stercoreus


cs2,0$
s"Club, Coral and Leafy"
gestion de clés mycologiques
Titre Fungi Growing on Wood/Key to Gilled Fungi / Key to Club, Coral, or Leafy Lettuce-like Fungi
Com$ tiré de http://www.messiah.edu/Oakes

1,2 $ Fruit bodies occurring either as 1) complex, lettuce-like masses of flattened and wavy-margined branchesor 2) complex, coral-like structures of erect worm-like stalks which branch repeatedly
1,6 $ Fruit bodies individually simple in appearance, occurring as single, upright, unbranched or sparingly branchedor forked structures which may or may not be tightly packed together
2,3 $ Branches flattened and wavy-margined, forming complex lettuce-like clusters
2,5 $ Branches not conspicuously flattened, forming complex, highly branched, coral-like clusters
3,0 $ Fruit body light to dark reddish brown $ Tremella foliacea
3,4 $ Fruit body white to pale yellow or pale tan
4,0 $ Deeply rooting dark brown to black stalk present; individual branches darkest attips $ Sparassis crispa
4,0 $ Rooting stalk absent; individual branches palest at tips $ Sparassis spathulata
5,0 $ Fruit body more compact, appearing to be formed of many highly branched, but parallel, upright stalks;branches pointed and without crown-like tips $ Ramaria spp.
5,0 $ Fruit body with open, candelabra--like structure; branches with crown-like tips $ Clavicorona pyxidata
6,7 $ Fruit body brown to black, at least in part
6,10 $ Fruit body various shades of white, yellow, orange or red; in no part black
7,0 $ Fruit bodies in dense clusters; individual fruit bodies delicate, without internal fleshy tissue, verythin (1-1.5 mm wide), up to 2 cm tall, and appearing as a cylindrical head on narrow, short,shiny, black stalk $ Stemonitis spp.
7,8 $ Fruit bodies single or clustered; individual fruit bodies greater than 1.5 mm wide, up to 8 cm tall,with internal fleshy tissue which may be quite tough or woody
8,0 $ Flesh inside brown to black; fruit body unbranched; upper club- like head elongate or not andusually compressed or having a longitudinal furrow, stalk velvety; stalk and head with blackspines (use hand lens) $ Trichoglossum hirsutum
8,9 $ Flesh inside white; fruit body branched or not; upper portions not compressed or having alongitudinal furrow. External surfaces of the following species may be partly white to buff whenimmature but are mostly black or blackish-brown.
9,0 $ Fruit body thick (1-3 cm wide); appearing as distorted, finger-like clubs $ Xylaria polymorpha
9,0 $ Fruit body thin (0.5-1 cm wide), forked, appearing as antler-like clubs $ Xylaria hypoxylon
10,11 $ Fruit bodies without structural strength, collapsing into a slimy material where touched; whiteto pinkish to red
10,12 $ Fruit bodies tougher than described above, maintaining structural integrity when touched; whitish orvariously colored
11,0 $ Fruit bodies whitish, appearing as scattered and clustered masses of tiny, translucent "icicles";individual erect fruit bodies 0.5-1 mm wide and 0.1-1 cm high;branched or simple $ Ceratiomyxa fruticulosa
11,35 $ Fruit bodies pinkish to reddish, appearing as densely crowded and compressed masseswith the appearance of raspberries; individual erect fruit bodies less than 0.5 mm wide and
12,0 $ Fruit body very tiny (1-1.5 mm wide, 3-10 mm tall), whitish; occurring on algae-covered,debarked logs $ Multiclavula mucida
12,13 $ Fruit body not as described above, typically larger, not whitish
13,14 $ Fruit body consists of a head-like portion borne on a stalk
13,18 $ Fruit body lacking a stalked head-like portion, occurring as upright cylindrical stalks withpointed tips, branched or not; yellow to yellow-orange; flesh tough and gelatinous
14,15 $ Head flattened longitudnally in some manner
14,16 $ Head cap-like, irregularly lobed and convoluted, margins strongly inrolled; not longitudnallycompressed
15,0 $ Head fan to spoon-shaped with wavy margins, cream colored to yellowish tobrownish yellow; stalk darker in color (ochre to reddish-brown), minutely downy,attached to substrate by orange hairs $ Spathulariopsis velutipes
15,0 $ Head spoon or tongue-shaped, compressed or longitudinally furrowed; stalkscaly-granular; head and stalk orange to yellow $ Microglossum rufum
16,0 $ Head dark greenish, stalk yellow to orange yellow $ Leotia viscosa
16,17 $ Head and stalk more or less the same color
17,0 $ Fruit body dull yellow to orange-yellow $ Leotia lubrica
17,0 $ Fruit body pea green to bluish green $ Leotia atrovirens
18,0 $ Fruit body 1-2.5 cm wide, 2.5-10 cm tall; branching from base with upper branchesusually forked $ Calocera viscosa
18,0 $ Fruit body up to 3 mm wide and up to 1.5 cm tall; unbranched or sometimes forkedat apex $ Calocera cornea
35,0 $ mm high $ Tubifera ferruginosa

