gestion de clés mycologiques
Titre Fungi Growing on Wood/Key to Gilled Fungi / / Key to Crust and Parchment Fungi
Com$ tiré de http://www.messiah.edu/Oakes
cs3,0$
s"Crust and Parchment"
1,2 $ Fruit body occurring as projecting shelf-like or petal-like caps (parchment-like in texture) with or withoutresupinate (crust-like) portions
1,6 $ Fruit body without projecting shelf or petal-like caps, appearing 1) as a sheetlike or crustlike spreading formor 2) as swollen, blackish spindle-like growths on twigs or 3) as closely arranged, broken pieces of whitishto grayish to tanish ceramic tile
2,0 $ Fertile resupinate surface brown with conical setae (use hand lens), surface smooth or cracked; capsbrownish and concentrically zoned; flesh turns blackish with KOH; fruit bodies often fuse laterally to formlarger colonies $ Hymenochaete tabacina complex
2,3 $ Fertile surface without conical setae; flesh not blackish with KOH
3,0 $ Cap pale gray/silvery and covered with tiny radiating silky fibers; caps 0.5-1 cm wide but often laterallyfused; fertile surface whitish, growing dead twigs and branches of hornbeam(Carpinus caroliniana) $ Stereum striatum
3,4 $ Cap typically hairy but color, size, and substrate not as above
4,0 $ Caps 1-7 cm wide; distinctly zoned with shades of dark brown, rust, and gray; fertile surface buff tocinnamon-buff; typically forming individual brackets rather than fusing $ Stereum ostrea
4,5 $ Caps 0.3-2 cm wide, zonate, often fused laterally
5,0 $ Cap covered with coarse, stiff hairs; fertile surface buff to gray $ Stereum hirsutum
5,0 $ Cap silky hairy, smooth and shiny near margin, radially furrowed; fertile surface orange but fadingto cinnamon-buff, slightly ridged where caps join $ Stereum complicatum
6,7 $ Fruit body sheetlike, crustlike, spreading in appearance
6,9 $ Fruit body as swollen, blackish spindle-like growths on twigs or as closely arranged, broken pieces of whitishto grayish to tanish ceramic tiles
7,0 $ Fruit body soft, fluffy, slimy, yellowish, spreading mass at first; maturing into dry, ochre to red-brownmass up to 20 cm long, 1-3 cm thick; with a brittle crust and blackish, powdery, dryspore mass underneath $ Fuligo septica
7,8 $ Fruit body hard, blackish, carbon-like
8,0 $ On decaying hardwood branches, often encircling them; surface finely roughened to nearlysmooth and shiny, sometimes cracked; flesh 1-1.5 mm thick $ Diatrype stigma
8,0 $ On stumps and roots of decaying hardwoods; surface irregular with bump-like pores giving finelyroughened appearance; flesh 3-6 mm thick; often brittle and easily detached $ Ustulina deusta
9,0 $ Fruit body as spindle-shaped or as knot-like swellings on cherry (Prunus) twigs andbranches $ Apiosporina morbosa
9,0 $ Fruit body hard and woody; appearing as closely arranged, small, broken pieces of whitishto pinkish-buff tiles typically on debarked oak logs and stumps $ Xylobolus frustulatus

cs4,0$
gestion de clés mycologiques
1,2 $ Fertile surface (inside of cup or if saucer-like, the upper surface) colored yellow or orange to orange-redor green or purplish
1,9 $ Fertile surface (inside of cup or if saucer-like, the upper surface) 1) whitish and concentrically zoned, 2) entirelywhitish or blackish, or 3) some shade of brown including tan, reddish brown or darker brown
2,3 $ Fruit body red, orange-red, orange-yellow to dull orange; margin fringed with conspicuous (use hand lens)dark brown or blackish hairs
2,4 $ Fruit body not with above combination of color and marginal dark hairs
3,0 $ Fruit body orange-yellow to dull orange; 1.5-5 mm wide $ Scutellinia erinaceus
3,0 $ Fruit body red, orange-red to orange; 3-15 mm wide $ Scutellinia scutellata
4,5 $ Fruit body green or yellow or purplish
4,7 $ Fruit body bright red
5,0 $ Fruit body green; colonized wood green $ Chlorociboria aeruginascens
5,6 $ Fruit body yellow or purplish
6,0 $ Fruit body lemon-yellow $ Bisporella citrina
6,0 $ Fruit body purplish $ Ascocoryne sarcoides
7,0 $ Cup-like fruit body borne on a stalk, stalk and cup covered with conspicuouswhite hairs $ Microstoma floccosa
7,8 $ Fruit body stalked or not; not covered with conspicuous white hairs
8,0 $ Shallow cup-like fruit body up to 1.3 cm wide; stalk conspicuous $ Sarcoscypha occidentalis
8,0 $ Shallow, cup-like fruit body 2-5 cm wide; stalk short or lacking $ Sarcoscypha austriaca
9,0 $ Fertile surface concentrically zoned, 0.5-2 cm wide $ Trametes conchifer
9,10 $ Fertile surface not zoned
10,11 $ Fertile surface whitish or blackish
10,14 $ Fertile some shade of brown including tan, reddish brown or darker brown
11,12 $ Fertile surface whitish
11,13 $ Fertile surface blackish
12,0 $ Fruit body larger (1-3 cm wide), outer surface covered with conspicuousbrownish hairs $ Humaria hemisphaerica
12,0 $ Fruit body very small (1 mm wide), stalked, and covered with conspicuouswhite hairs $ Dasyscyphus virgineus
13,0 $ Fruit body an urn-shaped cup, 3-8 cm wide; distinct stalk present $ Urnula craterium
13,0 $ Fruit body saucer-shaped to flat-topped at maturity, 2-4 cm wide; stalkless $ Bulgaria inquinans
14,15 $ Flesh of fruit body rubbery-gelatinous
14,16 $ Flesh of fruit body brittle, not gelatinous or rubbery; fruit body cup to saucer-shaped, and sometimesirregularly shaped with wavy or torn margins
15,0 $ Fruit body 1-3.5 cm wide, decidedly two-toned with the fertile surface tan to reddish brownand the outer surface blackish-brown and finely hairy $ Galiella rufa
15,0 $ Fruit body 3-15 cm wide, not distinctly two-toned; fruit body ear-shaped to irregularlycup-shaped with portions of the fruit body irregularly wrinkled or with raised, vein-likemarkings $ Auricularia auricula
16,0 $ Outer surface similar in color to inner (fertile) surface $ Peziza badioconfusa
16,0 $ Outer surface whitish $ Peziza repanda

