gestion de clés mycologiques
gestion de clés mycologiques
Titre Fungi Growing on Wood/Key to Gilled Fungi / Key to Poroid Fungi
Com$ tiré de http://www.messiah.edu/Oakes
1,2 $ Basidiocarp 1) almost globose; 1-3 cm diameter; on coniferous wood; pore surface coveredby partial veil or 2) basidiocarp extremely small (1-3 mm in diameter), pendent from a stalk-likebase
1,3 $ Basidiocarp not as above
2,0 $ Basidiocarp extremely small and pendent $ Porodisculus pendulus
2,0 $ Basidiocarp globose with pores covered by a partial veil $ Cryptoporus volvatus
3,4 $ Fertile surface superficially porelike but consisting of a network of shallow pits, wrinkles, andbranched folds with cross-veins
3,5 $ Fertile surface with 1) distinct round or angular pores, or 2) pores that form tooth-like structures, or 3)sinuous or winding pores very much longer in one direction (mazelike or daedaleoid), or 4) pores thatform gill-like (lamellate) structures
4,0 $ Upper surface of pileus coral pink, nearly glabrous; projecting up to 8 cm from substrate $ Phlebia incarnata
4,0 $ Upper surface of pileus white, wooly; projecting up to 1.5 cm from substrate $ Phlebia tremellosa
5,6 $ Fertile surface with pores that appear as toothlike, mazelike, or lamellate
5,7 $ Fertile surface with distinct round or angular pores
6,cs17$ Fertile surface as daedaleoid or lamellate $ Group B
6,cs16 $ Fertile surface toothlike $Group A 
7,cs18 $ Basidiocarp hard and woody when fresh and showing more than one layer of tubes (i.e. morethan one year’s growth) when cut vertically down the center (perennial polypores) $ Group C
7,8 $ Basidiocarps soft, fleshy, leathery, or corky when fresh and showing only one layer of tubeswhen cut down vertically down the center
8,9 $ Inner substance (context) yellowish brown or darker brown or decidedly pinkish, reddish,yellowish, or orange
8,10 $ Inner substance (context) white, whitish, pale yellow or very light tan
9,cs19 $ Context pinkish to reddish or yellowish or orange $Group D
9,cs20 $ Context yellowish brown or darker brown $Group E
10,cs21 $ Basidiocarp with a more or less distinct simple or branched stem $Group F 
10,11 $ Basidiocarp not stemmed (sessile)
11,12 $ Pore surface distinctly yellow or more or less smoke-colored or black
11,13 $ Pore surface not as above
12,0 $ Pore surface distinctly yellow $ Laetiporus sulphureus
12,cs22 $ Pore surface more or less smoke-colored or black $Group G
13,0$ Margin of pileus projecting downward 5 mm or more beyond the pore surface; found only on birch (Betula) $ Piptoporus betulinus
13,14 $ Margin not as above
14,0 $ Pileus surface more or less entirely brown or blackish; pores white,bruising brownish, flesh watery when young $ Ischnoderma resinosum
14,15 $ Pileus surface not uniformly brown in color
15,0 $ Basidiocarp firm and corky, fibrous or tough and leathery whenfresh $ Group I
15,cs24 $ Basidiocarp firm and corky, fibrous or tough and leathery whenfresh $ Group I




15,cs23 $ Basidiocarp soft and spongy in texture when fresh,context often watery $Group H $

cs8,0$
Titre Fungi Growing on Wood/Key to Gilled Fungi / Key to Puffball-shaped Fungi
Com$ tiré de http://www.messiah.edu/Oakes
1,2 $ Fruitbody small, 0.15-1.5 cm wide and high at maturity
1,4 $ Fruitbody typically larger than 1.5 cm wide and high at maturity
2,0 $ Fruitbody velvety; yellowish orange or paler with a yellowish orange hairy membrane(lid) covering the top. Removal of the membrane reveals a number of whitish “eggs” (peridioles) withinthe cup (“nest”) $ Crucibulum laeve
2,0,N$Note: Other species of bird’s nest fungi, when young, also have membranes covering the peridioles.
2,3 $ Fruitbody variously colored but not hairy; peridioles absent
3,0 $ Surface brick red to black, minutely bumpy; sectioning reveals hard, black flesh with minute cavities(perithecia) $ Hypoxylon fragiforme
3,0 $ Surface pinkish-gray or yellowish-brown or olivaceous-brown; sectioning reveals uniform pinkish, paste-likespore mass when immature, changing to powdery, ochre spore mass when mature $ Lycogala epidendrum
4,5 $ Fruitbody firm to the touch, hard or woody
4,7 $ Fruitbody fleshy or soft
5,0 $ Tough membrane present, covering pores of a poroid fruitbody; membrane eventually opensforming a hole; on conifers $ Cryptoporus volvatus
5,6 $ Membrane absent, not a poroid fungus
6,0 $ Fruitbody rounded or flattened; skin thick, tough, yellow-brown, and warted;interior purplish-black, marbled with whitish veins $ Scleroderma citrinum
6,0 $ Fruitbody rounded, surface blackish at maturity (grayish-white, then reddish-brown when immature),pimply; flesh blackish and concentrically zoned $ Daldinia concentrica
7,8 $ Fruitbody interior, when sectioned from top to bottom, revealing two or more internal chambersfilled with olive-green slime
7,10 $ Fruitbody interior without greenish slime in distinct chambers
8,0 $ Slime occurring as several (more than two) chambers in the upper portion of the fruit body;fruitbody pear-shaped to rounded, white to pinkish, attached to substrate at base by white topinkish rhizomorphs $ Phallogaster saccatus
8,9 $ Slime appearing as two chambers on either side of a central core of non slime tissue
9,0 $ Fruitbody, unsectioned, appearing egg-like (oval to pear-shaped); 3.5-6 cm tall and 3-4.5 cm wide;whitish to pinkish; attached to substrate by pinkish rhizomorphs $ Phallus ravenelii
9,0 $ Fruitbody, unsectioned, appearing egg-like (oval to round or pear-shaped),1-2 cm tall and 1-1.5 cmwide; white to pinkish-red; attached to substrate by white rhizomorphs $ Mutinus spp.
10,0 $ Interior whitish and spongy, often marbled with pinkish veins; exterior whitish; fruitbodies arebumpy, rounded to irregular masses usually found in association with grayish-brown mushroomswith pinkish gills and a whitish stalk $ Entoloma abortivum
10,11 $ Interior whitish and fleshy when immature, dry and powdery at maturiy; mature spore masspurple-brown or yellow to olive-brown
11,0 $ Spore mass purplish-brown when mature.. $ Morganella subincarnata
11,12 $ Spore mass greenish-brown when mature
12,0 $ Pear-shaped to nearly round; surface smoothish, yellow-brown, covered at first with minutewarts, granules or spines; opening with a pore on top; fruitbody attached to wood bywhite rhizomorphs $ Lycoperdon pyriforme
12,0 $ Round to turban-shaped, white, aging to yellow-brown; surface at first covered by longerconical spines with shorter spines and granules between, larger spines break off in age leavingdistinct round scars surrounded by short spines and granules.Opening by a pore on top $ Lycoperdon perlatum

