gestion de clés mycologiques
Titre Fungi Growing on Wood/Key to Gilled Fungi / groupe A :! Basidiocarp sessile or with a lateral stem.
Com$ tiré de http://www.messiah.edu/Oakes
1,0 $ Gill edges notched or toothed, like a saw; cap brownish, densely hairy $ Lentinellus ursinus
1,2 $ Gill edges even
2,0 $ Basidiocarps grayish black, very small (2-6 mm wide), found on undersides oflogs $ Resupinatus applicatus
2,3 $ Basidiocarps not black, typically larger, more prominently displayed
3,0 $ Spore print brown $ Crepidotus spp.
3,4 $ Spore print white, lilac-gray, pink or yellowish
4,5 $ Spore print white or lilac-gray
4,11 $ Spore print pink or yellowish
5,6 $ Cap conspicuously hairy or fuzzy
5,9 $ Cap smooth to minutely hairy or scaly, not distinctly hairy or fuzzy
6,0 $ Fruiting body larger (2.5-7.5 cm wide); cap pinkish-tan to reddish-brown or tinged violet, tanwith age $ Lentinus strigosus
6,7 $ Fruiting body small (1-3 cm wide); cap whitish, whitish-gray to brownish
7,0 $ True gills present, neither crimped nor split lengthwise; cap semi-circular or kidney-shaped;gills brownish to pale cinnamon; bitter taste $ Panellus stipticus
7,8 $ Gill-like folds either crimped or split lengthwise; folds whitish, gray or pink-tinged
